{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"duration_ms":59947,"num_turns":15,"result":"Done. `duration.py` now implements a general duration parser instead of the hard-coded `h`/`m` split.\n\nHow it works:\n- A shape regex requires one or more `amount unit` chunks (decimal amounts allowed, case-insensitive, optional whitespace between chunks and around the string), so empty strings, bare numbers (`\"10\"`), unknown units (`\"5x\"`), and negatives (`\"-5m\"`) fail immediately with `ValueError`.\n- Each chunk is then validated to be in strictly ascending unit order (`d` → `h` → `m` → `s`), which rejects repeats (`\"1h1h\"`) and out-of-order input (`\"5m1h\"`).\n- Amounts are converted with `d`=86400, `h`=3600, `m`=60, `s`=1 and summed; the result is rounded to the nearest second using floor(x + 0.5) so halves round up (avoiding Python's banker's rounding, e.g. `\"2.5s\"` → 3).\n\nValidation: the existing test in `tests/` passes (`python -m pytest -q` → 1 passed), and I ran an ad-hoc edge-case script covering every point in the issue: `\"90s\"`, `\"2h\"`, `\"1d2h\"`, `\"1h30m15s\"`, `\" 1h 5m \"`, `\"1.5h\"` → 5400, `\"0.5m\"` → 30, `\"2H\"` → 7200, plus the ValueError cases (`\"\"`, `\"5x\"`, `\"10\"`, `\"5m1h\"`, `\"1h1h\"`, negatives, trailing bare numbers like `\"1h5\"`, doubled units like `\"1m 5m\"`). All behaved as specified; no test files were touched.","session_id":"4e6e5cc3-8121-41f5-9b34-216e4f7beb61","usage":{"input_tokens":6267,"output_tokens":4155,"cache_read_input_tokens":168192,"cache_creation_input_tokens":0,"factory_credits":0,"thinking_tokens":0,"ttft_ms":1413.7333333333333}}
